#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116120} :activity/id #uuid "3b658871-23f8-5b8d-92ee-5294fec9bba3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445 :topic/id #uuid "a01c36d3-5643-5685-845e-29c37e89238f" :topic/math-academy-id 1733} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116493 :tutorial/content "We can use Gaussian elimination to find the general solutions of systems with any number of equations and variables. For instance, consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z=1, \\\\ y+3z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has the augmented matrix\n![](images/8a/8a8452587098eed9b697c3999c356359b949429f29ce16af2c81cf728be9480e.svg)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\, & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+4z=1 \\\\ y+3z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable $z.$ From the second equation, we get $y=-3z,$ and from the first equation, we obtain $x=1-4z.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-4z, \\\\ y=-3z, \\\\ z=z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1-4z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3eff050b-e4ba-52e5-aa10-7bba2feef2fc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3162 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6022e9b9-60ab-5ac5-b16a-17583fa649c3" :step/math-academy-id 17213 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116121}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116121 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115241 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115070 :question/id #uuid "dc6dd9b4-de7f-52d0-8ff9-a7a7f7a9abd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4711"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d81e3f1-d930-59ee-b439-2a3645aebb6c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048742}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417081}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Consistent System of Equations Using Gauss-Jordan Elimination"} :step/id #uuid "79bb6bd1-8c36-5f56-9575-8a1b8388eb57" :step/math-academy-id 17214 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116122}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116494 :tutorial/content "Recall that a system of linear equations is called **consistent** if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, if there are no solutions, the system is called **inconsistent**. \n\nFor a consistent system, \n\n- the system is called **consistent independent** if the solution is unique, while\n\n- the system is called **consistent dependent** if there are infinitely many solutions.\n\n![](images/f5/f5b808eac6e1d865b13b68ce2a4f7d317560ae3965928e478531fcd2148c2c6b.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e0c85152-daea-5a87-a3ba-81870558c7a2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3163 :tutorial/title "Consistent and Inconsistent Systems of Linear Equations"} :step/id #uuid "2fa6eba1-6298-51b5-bbc9-bd8c46a79468" :step/math-academy-id 17215 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116209}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115242 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115071 :question/id #uuid "f2c7cfd3-da43-5208-bd0e-21692357fabe" :question/math-academy-id "e-4712"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22733f71-de4a-5058-a41c-039e66e333c5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425949}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"} :step/id #uuid "1d1a97f8-c872-5285-b963-d1938948df37" :step/math-academy-id 17216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116209 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115124 :question/id #uuid "cd56314d-f45a-55be-9762-983fe507a1a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6173"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403787}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"} :step/id #uuid "e80cfcd9-1219-52d8-96c7-0ee06cdf52fc" :step/math-academy-id 23072 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116123}}] :activity/title "Gaussian Elimination For NxM Systems of Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115479}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425950} :activity/id #uuid "080f971c-621c-5ccf-b883-b98c49745b79" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445 :topic/id #uuid "a01c36d3-5643-5685-845e-29c37e89238f" :topic/math-academy-id 1733} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425950 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17602323-8777-51fe-9a70-fac173438e8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425938} {:answer/id #uuid "3745dd5b-bc8f-5c5f-bfbb-0a70d0ee8eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425940} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1862b9-030a-5755-b668-e59ca4c9e456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425944} {:answer/id #uuid "a1294db0-0da9-5a02-b9e5-791ef34dcf11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425946} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6dc1f0-4ead-53bf-8e9f-9b57805aa51d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425947}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425947} :answer-field/id #uuid "80130b9b-78a0-54cc-bf84-e036ce6c2ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "db005b4e-86cd-5194-b2fd-10ca4ee59c03" :question/math-academy-id "q-42021" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=3 \\\\ -3x-(k+1)y=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -(k+1) & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -(k+1) & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -k+5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2y=3 \\\\ -(k-5)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=5,$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+2y=3.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=5.$"} :step/id #uuid "2c3254d8-7342-5cba-aa85-c696386c710a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425952}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425951 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1eee681-520d-5d0a-802e-8d5b29c52974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438296} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ace7b5-89a9-5ece-ad6b-0ce43e80a049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438297}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438297} :answer-field/id #uuid "07e35039-20ea-5f8c-9889-aaf209c08f8c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439177} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c3d1f45-fc1a-5e10-a017-8a9199b3e295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438298} {:answer/id #uuid "039beb6d-4028-5582-9983-702125a74145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438299}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438298} :answer-field/id #uuid "168dcfad-47ff-5ee1-9214-61e870c730b0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439178} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae82757c-5e4b-593d-84dd-aca1302ca366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438300} {:answer/id #uuid "8f20a360-4b99-5b18-808c-9bab5fd09713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438301}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438300} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b7ded78-a4d6-5805-8398-330746f91418" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e80c83a9-9107-528b-af57-73287d15e8e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-42061" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & x-3y+z=0 \\\\  & -4x+6y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is consistent dependent\n\n- **II.** The system has no solution\n\n- **III.** The system is consistent independent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we can to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement I is true."} :step/id #uuid "3b9e4957-843e-5d56-8704-c60e6e8f032c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "359439f2-81e1-5ced-8482-b2cf08a71409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425939} {:answer/id #uuid "478b28da-a8a3-51e4-963d-8ac53adc010e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425941} {:answer/id #uuid "545c0eb7-c35a-5d44-b8e2-b045ff203715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425942} {:answer/id #uuid "684ddf9d-dd44-5397-b1a8-e6e3ca6a2f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425943} {:answer/id #uuid "9720657d-4e06-51f7-8e91-da074201b940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425941} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3a07998-8f4c-5e30-bdee-b2acbf389fbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "84cf8820-fa64-5d50-9d9c-d3a9404788a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-42059" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+3y=1 \\\\  & 6x+(k+3)y=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & k+3 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & k+3 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k-6 & -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y=1 \\\\ (k-6)y=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=6,$ then the second equation is $0=-8,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=6.$"} :step/id #uuid "e9dff08f-68e1-5fe8-9586-d7f8edd2598c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425951}}] :activity/title "Gaussian Elimination For NxM Systems of Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425937}]}
